  "voiceover": "A prisoner stood before the high priest. The witnesses had contradicted each other and the case was falling apart. Then the high priest asked him directly: are you the Christ, the Son of the Blessed? He answered: I am, and you will see the Son of Man seated at the right hand of Power, and coming with the clouds of heaven. He did not invent that language. He was fusing two texts at once — Daniel 7 and Psalm 110. Centuries before that trial, Daniel had seen a vision: with the clouds of heaven there came one like a son of man. In the Hebrew Bible, that is God's own imagery. He makes the clouds his chariot, says Psalm 104. The LORD is riding on a swift cloud, says Isaiah. And Daniel watches someone else arrive that way — not a second God, and not a creature either. One God, and the Son is God. Then Daniel says what the figure receives: dominion and glory and a kingdom, and all peoples, nations, and languages serve him. His dominion is an everlasting dominion. And that phrase — an everlasting dominion — is word for word what the book of Daniel says of the Most High himself. This is not a creature promoted. What the Son receives as man, he never lacked as God: truly God and truly man. And the earliest surviving Jewish readings of that vision took the figure to be one person, a coming messiah. Jesus was not inventing a reading. He was claiming one. Son of Man was his own name for himself, about eighty times in the Gospels, almost always on his own lips. And on the night it mattered most, he took the one Daniel saw on the clouds and said: that is me. The high priest did not ask what he meant. He tore his robes and called it blasphemy. No single title settles the question on its own; the case is cumulative. But this is the language Israel reserved for her God, and Jesus took it as his own — because it was. Those clouds were always his.",
